# Redirect rules for whichever host serves gficcrew.com.
#
# GitHub Pages reads NEITHER this file nor `_headers`, so the host no longer executes
# the rules below. They stay because they are now the single source of truth:
# scripts/prepare-gh-pages.mjs parses the 301/302 lines here and materialises each one
# as a real stub page (HTTP 200 + canonical + 0-second meta refresh) in the GitHub
# artifact. Editing a rule here still changes what gets deployed.
#
# The 404 rewrites at the bottom need no stub: GitHub Pages answers any unmatched path
# with the root 404.html, and /admin is kept out of the artifact entirely.

# The previous site served the Guangzhou page at /guangzhou. This build moved it under
# /guides/ -- keep the old link alive so inbound links and search results don't 404.
/guangzhou   /guides/guangzhou/  301
/guangzhou/  /guides/guangzhou/  301

# /homepage/ was a CMS placeholder ("Homepage content is managed via frontmatter fields
# above.") that leaked into the previous build and therefore into the sitemap that was
# submitted to Google. That route does not exist in this build, so without this rule the
# switch to a new host would turn an indexed URL into a 404. Send it to the homepage:
# that keeps the URL alive and consolidates a thin, content-free page into the real one.
/homepage   /  301
/homepage/  /  301

# The admin panel is served by the WorkBuddy cloud service and only accepts calls from
# its own origin (https://gficcrew.app.workbuddy.host). On gficcrew.com it would render
# a shell that cannot reach its backend, so answer 404 instead of shipping a dead page.
# Content management always happens at https://gficcrew.app.workbuddy.host/admin/
/admin      /404.html  404
/admin/*    /404.html  404
